# bonus3 → end

The program reads the end user's `.pass` file, then writes a NUL into the
password buffer at index `atoi(argv[1])`. It compares the truncated buffer
with `argv[1]` and executes `/bin/sh` on equality.

An empty argument makes `atoi("")` return zero. This sets the first byte
of the buffer to NUL, so the buffer and the empty argument compare equal.
No password guessing or memory corruption is needed.

From the bonus3 account on the RainFall VM:

```sh
printf 'id\ncat /home/user/end/.pass\n' | ./bonus3 ""
```

`disassemble main` shows the `atoi` result used as the NUL index and the
later `strcmp` branch. The VM run produced the end password in `flag`.
